* this is the first such publication, a measurement, widely respected and referred to by marxists
* the authors attempt to measure the effect of wage minimum increase
* the authors use the difference-in-difference method
	* they select 2 member states of the USA
		* one with a recent wage minimum increase
		* one without such
	* they survey data about the change in employment around a wage minimum increase
		* they narrow their survey to the restaurant sector of the economy
* the authors say that their findings are evidence that wage minimum does not destroy jobs
* no, it is not evidence, especially not against a natural simple intuitive theory
	* we do not have reason to believe that their numbers are genuine and accurate, but let us suppose that we were the ones who gathered the data
	* only 1-1 samples
		* this measurement contains only 1 sample from each group; because the samples are the 2 states
			* the employees can not be considered to be the samples, because they are dependent, they are under the same state, effected by the same laws
		* if in medicine somebody would suggest a 1-1 sampling measurement as scientific - that person would be unanimously considered to be a lunitic, not a scientist; but in economics this is treated okay, as economics is practiced not as science but as religion
		* though the authors mention that they had a few similar measurements about some other states in the past
	* the data is number of employees, but should be amount of worked time
	* the data does not take into account the changes in compensation types other than wages
	* the survey was telephone questioning of restaurant managers about their employments
		* the questioned managers may have been biased to answer the popular answer
	* the managers may have anticipated the wage minimum increase before the survey, then the wage minimum effect may have got realized before the survey
		* the plan to increase the wage minimum was announced 2 years before it became law
	* the sampling is very thin, as it is restricted to the restaurants; many possible types of changes for restaurants influence their employments
		* wealth effect
			* if people are more rich then they buy more service, including restaurant service
		* tax or other burdon decrease on restaurants
	* at low wage level the effect of the wage minimum increase really is little, may well be within normal measurement accuracy
* if this work is to be considered as scientifically relevant then it is not in economics but in psychology, as example of the phenomenon that people (who are considered (by themselves and many others) to be scientists in the topic) invent and believe in and spread propositions which are obviously false
* David Card seems to have recieved a Nobel prize for usage of empirical methods in labour economics
